<!-- HISTORICAL CONTEXT & MEDICAL AFTERWORD BOX             -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->

<div class="historical-box">
  <strong>HISTORICAL &amp; FORENSIC DOSSIER &mdash; BULOVKA TO LIDICE:</strong><br><br>
  
  <strong>1. The Pursuit:</strong> Contemporary police reports, eyewitness depositions, and the official Gestapo investigation (the <em>Pannwitz Report</em>) confirm that Heydrich was not immobilized in the vehicle. Driven by acute adrenaline, he exited the shattered car and chased Jozef Gabčík uphill for 15 to 20 paces, firing his Walther PPK until his weapon failed and internal hemorrhage caused immediate circulatory collapse.<br><br>
  
  <strong>2. The Medical Reality (&ldquo;The Hidden Insult&rdquo;):</strong> Transported to Prague’s Bulovka Hospital in the flatbed of a baker's delivery van, Heydrich was operated on by German surgeons Dr. Josef Hohlbaum and Dr. Walter Dick under the supervision of Himmler's personal physician, Karl Gebhardt. While popular history often attributes his death on June 4, 1942, to exotic bacterial toxins or sepsis caused by upholstery horsehair driven into his torn spleen, modern forensic re-examinations suggest a classic &ldquo;hidden insult&rdquo;: massive delayed internal bleeding (retroperitoneal hemorrhage) combined with acute pulmonary embolism seven days after trauma, an agonizing physiological breakdown that no medical intervention of the era could arrest.<br><br>
  
  <strong>3. The Price of the Myth (Lidice, June 10, 1942):</strong> Heydrich’s death triggered the savage logic of totalitarian reprisal. Acting under Hitler’s direct order to &ldquo;wade through blood,&rdquo; the SS surrounded the mining village of <strong>Lidice</strong> on the night of June 9. On <strong>June 10</strong>, all 173 men and boys over fifteen were executed against a barn wall; 184 women were deported to Ravensbrück; 88 children were murdered in the gas vans of Chełmno. The village was systematically burned, dynamited, and bulldozed until it was erased from the map.<br><br>

  <strong>4. Lina Heydrich and the Reprisals:</strong> While Lina Heydrich held no official command role and was never consulted by the SS leadership—consumed at the time by the state funeral, immediate grief, and late pregnancy (giving birth to Marte six weeks later)—it is historically certain she was aware of the destruction of Lidice. Unlike the death camps, Lidice was not a state secret; the Nazi regime openly boasted of the massacre on the radio and across Prague's front pages as a deliberate act of public terror. In her postwar memoirs, Lina fiercely compartmentalized the atrocity: because Reinhard died six days before the village was razed, she steadfastly dismissed Lidice as Hitler’s personal revenge, disclaiming any moral connection between her family and the slaughter.
</div>
